The cleanest way to handle dialogue is to treat a "scene" as an object that manages a list of **DialogNodes**. Each node contains the text to display and a list of available choices.

#### 1. Create the Data Structures
First, define what a Choice and a DialogueNode look like.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

// Represents a single line of dialogue or a decision point
class DialogueNode {
    String text;
    List<Choice> choices = new ArrayList<>();
    String nextNodeId; // Used to determine where to go after a choice

    public DialogueNode(String text) {
        this.text = text;
        this.nextNodeId = ""; 
    }
}

// Represents a clickable option
class Choice {
    String prompt; // The text the user sees (e.g., "Yes")
    String nextNode; // The ID of the node to go to
    String action;   // Optional: "combat_start", "unlock_door", etc.

    public Choice(String prompt, String nextNode) {
        this.prompt = prompt;
        this.nextNode = nextNode;
        this.action = null;
    }

    public Choice(String prompt, String nextNode, String action) {
        this.prompt = prompt;
        this.nextNode = nextNode;
        this.action = action;
    }
}
```

